<section class="w3-signs" id="leak-signs" aria-labelledby="w3-signs-title">
  <div class="w3-signs-inner">
    <header class="w3-signs-intro">
      <p class="w3-signs-kicker"><span aria-hidden="true">●</span> HOMEOWNER’S FIELD GUIDE <i>HOUSTON, TX</i></p>
      <h2 id="w3-signs-title">A hidden leak often leaves <em>small clues.</em></h2>
      <p class="w3-signs-lead">A higher bill, damp wall, or wet patch can have more than one cause. Look for patterns and note what changes.</p>
      <div class="w3-signs-reminder">
        <span class="w3-reminder-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 3c-3 4-6 7.2-6 11a6 6 0 0 0 12 0c0-3.8-3-7-6-11Z"/><path d="M9.5 14a2.5 2.5 0 0 0 2.5 2.5"/></svg></span>
        <span><strong>A clue is not a diagnosis.</strong><small>These signs can also come from appliances, irrigation, roof leaks, or condensation.</small></span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="w3-sign-notes" role="list" aria-label="Signs and practical first checks for possible leaks">
      <div class="w3-notes-heading"><span>WHAT YOU NOTICE</span><span>WHAT TO CHECK</span></div>

      <article class="w3-note-row" role="listitem">
        <span class="w3-note-number">01</span>
        <div class="w3-note-main"><h3>Water bill went up</h3><p>A running toilet, irrigation, appliance, or hidden leak can increase water use.</p></div>
        <p class="w3-note-check"><b>Compare bills.</b> Check toilets, faucets, and irrigation.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="w3-note-row" role="listitem">
        <span class="w3-note-number">02</span>
        <div class="w3-note-main"><h3>Water sound with fixtures off</h3><p>A hiss or trickle may mean water is flowing; automatic appliances can also run.</p></div>
        <p class="w3-note-check"><b>Pause known water use.</b> Listen again and check whether the meter moves.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="w3-note-row" role="listitem">
        <span class="w3-note-number">03</span>
        <div class="w3-note-main"><h3>Damp wall or ceiling</h3><p>Stains, peeling paint, or a musty smell may come from plumbing, roof entry, or condensation.</p></div>
        <p class="w3-note-check"><b>Take a photo.</b> Note if it changes after rain or water use.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="w3-note-row" role="listitem">
        <span class="w3-note-number">04</span>
        <div class="w3-note-main"><h3>Warm or wet floor</h3><p>Sunlight or appliances can warm floors; recurring dampness may have another source.</p></div>
        <p class="w3-note-check"><b>Mark the spot.</b> Note whether it is warm, wet, or both.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="w3-note-row" role="listitem">
        <span class="w3-note-number">05</span>
        <div class="w3-note-main"><h3>Soggy patch outside</h3><p>Rain, drainage, irrigation, or a buried water line can leave ground unusually wet.</p></div>
        <p class="w3-note-check"><b>Check rain and irrigation.</b> See whether the area stays wet when they are off.</p>
      </article>

      <p class="w3-notes-foot">If a sign persists, share when it began and what you have checked with a leak detection professional.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

